<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_fourth-examples" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Examples</title>
  
  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Chose one of the following that does not affect an increase in temperature, if the body is heated.
        <ol marker="a">
          <li>Mass of the body</li>
            <li>State (solid, liquid, or gas) of the body</li>
              <li>Shape of the body</li>
                <li>Types of material (e.g., iron, aluminum, wood, etc.) of the body</li>
                  </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
     <answer>
      <p>
        <m>a.</m>
      </p>
    </answer>   
  </example>

   <example>
    <p>
    From the common house hold materials below, which one has the highest specific heat capacity? 
     <ol marker="a">
        <li>Water</li>
          <li>Iron</li>
            <li>Ice</li>
              <li>Sand</li>
                </ol>
      </p>  
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>a.</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
  </example>
  

  <example>
    <statement>
    <p>
    How much heat is needed to boil 500 grams of water initially at <m>20\,^oC</m>. 
    The specific heat capacity of water is <m>1 \,cal/g.^oC</m>. 
    </p>
  </statement>
   <solution>
    <p>
      Given: <m>m= 500\, g</m>, <m>t_i=20\,^o C</m>, <m>t_f=100 \,^o C</m>, <m>c=1 \,cal/g.^o C</m>, <m>Q=?</m>. 
    </p>
    <p>From equation <xref ref="subsubsec-sh"/>, we have
      <md>
        <mrow>Q \amp =mc(T_f-T_i) </mrow>
        <mrow>or,\quad Q \amp =500 \,g\times 1\, cal/g.^o C\times (100-20)\,^oC </mrow>
        <mrow>or,\quad Q \amp =40000 \,cal = 40000\times 4.2 \,J/cal =  9523 \,J</mrow>
      </md>
      </p>
   </solution>
  </example>

   <example>
    <p>
    Chose one of the following which is independent on the size and shape of the material composed in a body.
     <ol marker="a">
        <li>Density</li>
          <li>Volume</li>
            <li>Mass</li>
              <li>Weight</li>
                </ol>
      </p>  
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>a.</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
  </example>


   <example>
    <p>
    Some objects of identical volumes are being compared, the one with the greatest mass also has the greatest
     <ol marker="a">
          <li>temperature</li>
            <li>buoyancy</li>
              <li>pressure</li>
                <li>density</li>
                </ol>
      </p>  
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>d.</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
  </example>

<example>
    <p>
    The cake of soap sinks in water, the buoyant force on the soap is 
     <ol marker="a">
          <li>equal to its weight.</li>
            <li>higher than its weight.</li>
              <li>less than its weight.</li>
                <li>zero.</li>
                </ol>
      </p>  
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>c.</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
  </example>


  <example>
    <p>
    What is the mass of water that can be stored in an aquarium of 60 cm long, 30 cm wide, 
    and 20 cm high. The density of water is <m>1 \,g/ml.</m>
     </p>  
      <hint>
        <p>
          Find the volume of the container and calculate the mass using mass, volume, and density relationship.
        </p>
      </hint>
  </example>


<example>
    <p>
    Ice floats in water because 
     <ol marker="a">
          <li>it is colder than water.</li>
            <li>its density is less than that of water.</li>
              <li>its specific heat is less than that of water.</li>
                <li>it is a solid and water is a liquid.</li>
                </ol>
      </p>  
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>b.</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
  </example>

<example>
    <p>
    Molecular motion of the substance is least possible at which temperature? 
     <ol marker="a">
          <li><m>0 \,^o C</m>.</li>
            <li><m>0\, K</m>.</li>
              <li><m>-273 \,K</m>.</li>
                <li><m>0 \,^o F</m>.</li>
                </ol>
      </p>  
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>b.</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
  </example>

<example>
    <p>
    Select one of the following in which heat transfer occurs by conduction 
     <ol marker="a">
          <li>only in solids.</li>
            <li>only in liquids.</li>
             <li>only in solids and liquids.</li>
                <li>in solids, liquids, and gases.</li>
                </ol>
      </p>  
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>d.</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
  </example>

<example>
    <p>
   At which temperature does a Celsius thermometer and a Fahrenheit thermometer show the same readings? 
     <ol marker="a">
          <li>40 <m>^o C</m>.</li>
            <li>-40 <m>^o C</m>.</li>
             <li>40 <m>^o F</m>.</li>
                <li>0 <m>^o C</m>.</li>
                </ol>
      </p>  
      <solution>
        <p>
          Consider <m>F=C=x</m>, then from <xref ref="eqn-tempconv"/>, we have
          <md>
            <mrow>\frac{F-32}{9}  \amp = \frac{C}{5}</mrow>
            <mrow>or, \frac{x-32}{9}  \amp = \frac{x}{5}</mrow>
          </md>
          Solve this equation to find the value of x.
        </p>
      </solution>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>b.</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
  </example>

<example>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Which of the following is not a basic state of matter?
      <ol marker="a">
        <li>Solid</li>
          <li>Liquid</li>
           <li>Gas</li>
              <li>Plasma</li>
              </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      d.
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>

<example>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Which of the following is an example of a <term>chemical change?</term> [read <xref ref="sec_eighth-intro"/>]
      <ol marker="a">
        <li>Ice melting</li>
          <li>Salt dissolving in water</li>
           <li>Rust forming on iron</li>
              <li>Sugar dissolving in tea</li>
              </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      c.
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>

<example>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Which of the following is an example of a <term>physical change</term>? [read <xref ref="sec_eighth-intro"/>]
      <ol marker="a">
        <li>Cooking an egg</li>
          <li>Burning a piece of paper</li>
           <li>Crushing a can</li>
              <li>Digesting food</li>
              </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      c.
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>


<example>
  <statement>
    <p>
      What is the relationship between temperature and the average 
      kinetic energy of particles in a substance?
      <ol marker="a">
        <li>They are unrelated.</li>
          <li>They are directly proportional.</li>
           <li>They are inversely proportional.</li>
              <li>They are only related in gases.</li>
              </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      b.
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>


<example>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Which of the following is not a type of fluid?
      <ol marker="a">
        <li>Gas.</li>
          <li>Liquid.</li>
           <li>Solid.</li>
              <li>Plasma.</li>
              </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      c.
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>


<example>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Which of the following is a characteristic of fluids?
      <ol marker="a">
        <li>They have definite shape.</li>
          <li>They have definite volume.</li>
           <li>They are compressible.</li>
              <li>They can flow.</li>
              </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      d.
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>


<example>
  <statement>
    <p>
      What is buoyancy?     
      <ol marker="a">
        <li> The ability of an object to sink in a fluid.</li>
          <li> The ability of an object to float in a fluid.</li>
           <li> The force exerted by a fluid on an object submerged in it.</li>
              <li> The force exerted by an object on a fluid.</li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      c.
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>


<example>
  <statement>
    <p>
      What is Archimedes' principle?
      <ol marker="a">
        <li> The buoyant force on an object is equal to the weight of the object.</li>
          <li>The buoyant force on an object is equal to the weight of the fluid displaced.</li>
           <li>The buoyant force on an object is equal to the volume of the object.</li>
              <li>The buoyant force on an object is equal to the density of the fluid.</li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      b.
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>


<example>
  <statement>
    <p>
      What is the buoyant force?
      <ol marker="a">
        <li>  The force that pulls an object down when it is submerged in a fluid.</li>
          <li>The force that pushes an object up when it is submerged in a fluid.</li>
           <li>The force that opposes the motion of an object in a fluid.</li>
              <li>The force that causes a fluid to flow.</li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      b.
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>


<example>
  <statement>
    <p>
      What is the relationship between the pressure of a gas and the temperature of the gas, 
      according to Charles's law?
      <ol marker="a">
        <li>  They are directly proportional.</li>
          <li>They are inversely proportional.</li>
           <li>They are not related.</li>
              <li>It depends on the volume of the gas.</li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      a.
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>

</section>
